  "worked_solution": "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side,  factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+10x+12 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+10x & =-12 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+10x & =-12 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}+5x) & =-12 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}x+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}] & =-12 \\\\ 2[{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{4}] & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{25}{4} & =-12 \\\\ 2{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{2} & =-12 \\\\ 2{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =-12+\\frac{25}{2} \\\\ 2{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{5}{2}.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\ |x+\\frac{5}{2}| & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x+\\frac{5}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{6}{3}=-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{4}{2}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-3$ and $x=-2.$",
